+/// How plausible `word` is as one word of a Mad Gab *answer*, in
+/// [0, 1].
+///
+/// A Mad Gab answer is a phrase a person can say out loud and a solver can
+/// recognise, so each of its words has to be a real lexical item that
+/// stands on its own.  Length is the cheap, monotone test for that: a
+/// one- or two-letter token is not a plausible English content word, and
+/// the bare phonetic fragments the approximate search otherwise loves best
+/// — `mm`, `ad`, `p`, `pa` — are all of them.  Nearly every two-letter
+/// English token is already a function word, so this mostly reinforces the
+/// closed-class test, but it needs no word list to do it.
+///
+/// A closed-class word is scored `CLOSED_CLASS_QUALITY` rather than by
+/// its length.  Both factors punish the same words — almost every
+/// one- and two-letter English token is a function word — so applying
+/// both double-counts, and the double count is what pushed `wreck a nice
+/// beach` (one function word in four) out of the visible list.  Which of
+/// the two factors a word is charged for has to be decided by what it
+/// *is*; the other one is left to the closed-share discount, which is
+/// convex and therefore nearly free at a low share.
+///
+/// It replaces `lexical_shape_quality`, which asked the same question and
+/// got it wrong: it was a step function that scored a one-letter `a` at
+/// 1.0, exactly as high as a seven-letter `justice`.  Over the 2000 best
+/// candidates of a dozen real targets its range was 0.010-0.030 of a 0.05
+/// weight, so it could not separate anything in any case.
+fn answer_word_quality(word: &str) -> f64 {
+    if lexical::is_closed_class(word) {
+        return CLOSED_CLASS_QUALITY;
+    }
+    let mut letters = 0usize;
+    for c in word.chars() {
+        if c.is_alphanumeric() {
+            letters += 1;
+        }
+    }
+    match letters {
+        0 | 1 => 0.0,
+        2 => 0.4,
+        3 => 0.85,
+        _ => 1.0,
+    }
+}
